consider a + b * c being parsed by a combination of recursive and iterative methods

parse_expression(PREC_MINIMUM)
    parse_leaf gives a
    next token is operator so the expr is not over yet
    prev precedence (PREC_MINIMUM) is less than current precedence (PREC_ADD)
    create infix expression { a, +, parse_expression("b * c", PREC_ADD) }
        parse_leaf gives b
        next token is operator so expr is not over yet
        prev precedence (PREC_ADD) is less than current prec (PREC_MULT)
        create infix expression { b, *, parse_expression("c", PREC_MULT)}
            parse_leaf gives c
            end of line is not operator so no loop
        return infix expression { b, *, c }
    return infix expression { a + { b * c } }



consider a similar a * b + c being parsed the same way

parse_expression(PREC_MINIMUM)
    parse_leaf gives a
    next token is operator so expr is not over yet
    prev prec (MIN) is less than next prec (MULT) so enter loop
    create infix expression { a, *, parse_expression("b + c", PREC_MULT) }
        parse_leaf gives b
        next token is operator so expr is not over yet
        prev prec (MULT) is not less than next prec (ADD) so don't enter loop at all
        return leaf (b)
    infix expression { a, *, b } 
    next token (+) is operator so not over yet
    prev prec (MIN) is less than next prec (ADD) so enter loop
	create infix expression { { a * b } , +, parse_expression("c", PREC_ADD) }
		parse_leaf gives c
		next token is not operator, return c as expression
	return infix expression { { a * b }, +, "c" }
